In April 2025, Microsoft started requiring the color camera to see a visible face when using Windows Hello. This is for security reasons, but it causes Windows Hello to stop working in low light. 

I don't need this enhanced security, so I finally got around to creating this service to turn off the RGB camera when the user logs out or locks the computer. This allows you to sign in with the IR camera alone, which works faster and in all lighting (or lack thereof) conditions.

The camera is re-enabled when the user logs in.

DO NOT USE this application if you are not OK with the downgraded security. 

To use:
  1. Extract the release zip to a directory of your choosing.
  2. Navigate to that directory and right-click "install.cmd" and choose "Run as administrator"

You can use uninstall.cmd (as admin) to uninstall the service and then manually delete the install directory.

Check that the camera string in the config file matches your color camera in Device Manager. 
For Example:
	- On Alienware M16 R1s  the IR cameras is called "Integrated IR Webcam" and the color camera is "Integrated Webcam"
	- On Dell XPS 13 (9310)s, the camers are both called "Integrated Webcam"

If your computer names the IR and color cameras exactly the same (as in the XPS 13), this program will guess that the device instance ID ending in all 0s is the root color camera and disable that one. 

If that doesn't work, specify a device instance ID for the camera you need to disable. Unfortunately, I am not sure how to find it except by trial and error (disable it, try Windows Hello in low-light conditions, rinse and repeat). It is called "Device Instance Path" in Device Manager. Add this ID to the config.
